## 15. Tool-specific configuration locations

{{#is_claude_code}}
Precedence (highest first): managed settings > command-line `--settings` > `.claude/settings.local.json` > `.claude/settings.json` > `~/.claude/settings.json`. List keys such as permission rules merge across files, and a deny anywhere beats an allow anywhere. Managed sources are ranked server-managed > MDM (plist / HKLM) > managed files (`managed-settings.json`, then `managed-settings.d/*.json` in alphabetical order, later files replacing scalars and lists being combined) > HKCU; under the default `managedSourcesBehavior` `"first-wins"` only the highest source that delivers a policy is used, and `/status` names the skipped ones.

| Location | Scope | What to record |
|---|---|---|
| macOS `/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode/managed-settings.json`; Linux / WSL `/etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json`; Windows `C:\Program Files\ClaudeCode\managed-settings.json`; drop-ins in `managed-settings.d/*.json` beside it; `managed-mcp.json` in the same directory | managed (file) | Full content and SHA-256; file owner and permissions |
| macOS managed preferences domain `com.anthropic.claudecode`; Windows `HKLM\SOFTWARE\Policies\ClaudeCode`, value `Settings` (JSON). `HKCU\SOFTWARE\Policies\ClaudeCode` is user-writable and not an admin source | managed (MDM) | Profile / registry export and SHA-256 |
| claude.ai admin console: Organization settings > Claude Code > Managed settings (cached locally in `~/.claude/remote-settings.json`) | managed (server) | Export or screenshot; owner who changed it. The vendor classes this as a client-side control only; it is skipped with third-party cloud providers or a custom base URL, and not delivered to Console API-key sessions (typical in CI) |
| `managedSourcesBehavior` (`"first-wins"` default, or `"merge"`) | managed | Which managed sources are combined; `/status` "Skipped sources" |
| `~/.claude/settings.json` | user | Content and SHA-256 on reference machines |
| `.claude/settings.json`, `.claude/settings.local.json`, `.mcp.json` | repository / local | Content and SHA-256 |
| Managed `CLAUDE.md` (`/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode/CLAUDE.md`, `/etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md`, `C:\Program Files\ClaudeCode\CLAUDE.md`) | managed | SHA-256 |
| `~/.claude/projects/<project>/<session>.jsonl`, `~/.claude/history.jsonl` | local records | Retention (`cleanupPeriodDays`), access |
| `anthropics/claude-code-action` step (`claude_args`, `settings` inputs) and the app used | CI | Pinned SHA, inputs, app permissions |
| Organisation switches for Remote Control and cloud sessions (admin console); per device `disableRemoteControl` | organisation | State |

Keys to record (managed unless stated):

- Permission mode: `permissions.defaultMode`, `permissions.disableBypassPermissionsMode` (`"disable"`), `disableAutoMode` (`"disable"`). Auto mode, in which a classifier approves actions, is the built-in starting mode for interactive sessions in recent versions, so set the mode explicitly.
- Rules: `permissions.allow` / `ask` / `deny`, `allowManagedPermissionRulesOnly`, `permissions.blockReadsOutsideWorkingDirectories`.
- Hooks: `hooks` (with `"onFailure": "block"` on policy hooks), `allowManagedHooksOnly`.
- MCP: `allowManagedMcpServersOnly`.
- Sandbox: `sandbox.enabled`, `sandbox.failIfUnavailable`, `sandbox.allowUnsandboxedCommands`, `sandbox.network.allowedDomains`, `sandbox.network.allowManagedDomainsOnly`, `sandbox.filesystem.denyRead`, `sandbox.filesystem.allowManagedReadPathsOnly`, `sandbox.credentials`.
- Models: see section 12.
- Login and provider: `forceLoginMethod`, `forceLoginOrgUUID`, `allowedProviders`, `forceRemoteSettingsRefresh`.
- Versions: `requiredMinimumVersion`, `requiredMaximumVersion`, `autoUpdatesChannel`, `env.DISABLE_UPDATES`.
- Provenance: `attribution` (`commit`, `pr`, `sessionUrl`); set it in managed settings so instruction files cannot change it.
- Records and data: `cleanupPeriodDays`, `env.CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_TELEMETRY` and `OTEL_*` variables (including `OTEL_LOG_MANAGED_SETTINGS`), `env.CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_NONESSENTIAL_TRAFFIC`, `env.DISABLE_FEEDBACK_COMMAND`.
- Built-in plugins and extensions: `pluginConfigs` (for example instruction-file selection), and the managed restriction of user-installed mods: `pluginConfigs` `cc-plugin-sec-default@builtin` option `allowManagedModsOnly` true together with `disableSideloadFlags` true (recommended for developer machines); `allowManagedHooksOnly` (recommended for CI and agent runners, where user hooks, status lines and similar conveniences are not needed). Record which extensions are allowed.

Effective-configuration evidence: `/status` ("Setting sources", "Skipped sources"), `claude doctor`, and the `claude_code.managed_settings_resolved` telemetry event with `managed_settings.resolved_sha256` (identical digests mean identical policy).
{{/is_claude_code}}
{{#is_codex}}
Codex has two layers: **defaults** that users can override (config files, profiles, legacy managed defaults) and **admin requirements** that users cannot override. When a user value conflicts with a requirement, the client falls back to an allowed value and notifies the user.

| Location | Scope | What to record |
|---|---|---|
| `/etc/codex/requirements.toml` (Unix including macOS); `%ProgramData%\OpenAI\Codex\requirements.toml` (Windows) | requirements (system) | Content, SHA-256, owner and permissions |
| Cloud-managed requirements (admin console, Agent Security / managed configurations; targets by group, user, platform) | requirements (cloud) | Export or screenshot of policy, targets and raw TOML |
| `/etc/codex/managed_config.toml` (legacy; its fields are reinterpreted as requirements, so a "default" also acts as an allowlist) | requirements (legacy) | Content and SHA-256 |
| macOS MDM domain `com.openai.codex`: key `requirements_toml_base64` (requirements, highest priority) and key `config_toml_base64` (managed defaults) | MDM | Decoded payload and SHA-256 |
| `/etc/codex/config.toml` (Windows `%ProgramData%\OpenAI\Codex\config.toml`) | system defaults | Content and SHA-256 |
| `~/.codex/config.toml` (`CODEX_HOME`); profile files `~/.codex/<name>.config.toml` | user | Content and SHA-256 on reference machines |
| `<repo>/.codex/config.toml`, `.codex/hooks.json`, rules (loaded only for trusted projects) | repository | Content and SHA-256 |
| `[projects."<path>"] trust_level` in user config | user | Trust state of each repository |
| `~/.codex/hooks.json`; `rules/` folders next to config layers (for example `~/.codex/rules/default.rules`) | user | Content and SHA-256 |
| `~/.codex/auth.json` or OS keyring (`cli_auth_credentials_store`) | credentials | Storage mode (no plaintext file when keyring is required) |
| `~/.codex/history.jsonl`, `~/.codex/sessions`, `log_dir` | local records | Retention settings, access |
| `openai/codex-action` step (`permission-profile`, `safety-strategy`, `codex-version`, `allow-users`, `allow-bots`) | CI | Pinned tag or SHA and inputs |

Keys to record:

- Approvals: `approval_policy` (`"on-request"`, `"never"` or the granular table) and requirements `allowed_approval_policies`; `approvals_reviewer` and `allowed_approvals_reviewers` (keep `"user"` so a person decides every escalation).
- Sandbox: `sandbox_mode` (`read-only`, `workspace-write`, `danger-full-access`) and `[sandbox_workspace_write]` (`writable_roots`, `network_access`), or permission profiles (`default_permissions`, `[permissions.<name>]`; beta) with requirements `allowed_sandbox_modes` and `allowed_permission_profiles`. If `sandbox_mode` appears in any loaded config, the legacy settings win over profiles.
- Rules and reads: requirements `[rules] prefix_rules` (decision `"prompt"` or `"forbidden"`), `[permissions.filesystem] deny_read`.
- Hooks: `allow_managed_hooks_only`, `[features] hooks = true` in requirements; non-managed hooks must be trusted by hash.
- MCP: requirements `[mcp_servers]` allowlist by name and identity (an empty table disables all servers).
- Network and web: `web_search` and `allowed_web_search_modes`.
- Environment: `shell_environment_policy` (`inherit`, `ignore_default_excludes`, `filters`).
- Login: requirements `allowed_login_methods`, `allowed_chatgpt_workspaces`, `cli_auth_credentials_store` (honoured only in the local system file or MDM); user `forced_login_method`, `forced_chatgpt_workspace_id`.
- Data: `enforce_residency`, `history.persistence`, `[otel]` (an OTLP exporter is a table with endpoint and protocol), `analytics.enabled`, `feedback.enabled`, `check_for_update_on_startup`.
- Models: see section 12.

Effective-configuration evidence: `codex --strict-config doctor`, `codex features list`, `codex login status`, `/status` and `/hooks` in the TUI, and the `codex.conversation_starts` telemetry event (records sandbox and approval policy).
{{#surf_cloud_agent}}

Cloud: record the workspace roles for cloud use and environment management, each environment's internet access and domain allowlist, and who can use each shared environment. Cloud environments do not inherit local device policy or MDM.
{{/surf_cloud_agent}}
{{/is_codex}}
